chileans have elected the leftist former  student protest leader gabriel borrich  as their new president following one of  the most polarizing elections in decades  there supporters of the progressive  social democrats celebrated the victory  in santiago de chile boric ran on a  platform in support of public education  health care and equal rights for  indigenous and lgbtq people his opponent  farm right ultra conservative jose  antonio cast phone borrich and conceded  defeat cast was seen as the law and  order candidate  let's go straight to dw correspondent  benjamin alvarez gruber who's covering  the election for us benjamin you're in  downtown santiago how is the victory of  gabrielle borrich being received in the  chilean capital  [Music]  good afternoon anthony there are still  people around here in the capital  santiago there is still music on stage  the president-elect gabriel boris  already addressed the crowd there are  people celebrating they are singing and  of course they are happy that after jose  antonio caste farad candidate that you  just mentioned won the first round now  it's the turn of the former student  protest leader there were a lot of  people coming to the center of uh of  this of the stage that's right nearby in  the center of santiago and we were able  to talk to some of his supporters who  came here to celebrate that he will be  the next president of chile let's listen  i'm very happy and full of hope now  change can happen change that we have  wanted for more than two years or for 30  years  i'm very emotional this is a historic  moment like nothing that has ever  happened there's no precinct in the  history of chile it's truly emotional  we've made so much progress now we will  move forward with social rights continue  to advance and fight against the far  right  i feel tremendous joy i was here when i  was 15 years old celebrating the defeat  of pinochet now again we see that hope  is back hope for a more united and just  country even with all the things that  remain to be done  some chilean voters there reacting to  the victory of gabrielle borac talk to  us a little bit more about the man  gabriel burrich benjamin what does he  stand for  he stands for to continue the work of  this constitutional assembly that was  drafted after the massive protest the  rock chile in 2019 with a severe human  rights crisis in this country he talked  to the victims of this human rights  violation saying that they will be  investigated that the perpetrators will  be punished he is also in favor of  including the indigenous population it  will be one of the central points of  this constitutional assembly that is  already drafting a new constitution that  will replace the constitution that dates  back to 1980 at the time of the  dictatorship of augusto pinochet we just  heard one man talking about the pinochet  dictatorship as well in memories that he  has today and it shows that the  dictatorship is also an important day  today not only because gabrielle bourage  also wants to see this human rights  abuse as punished but also because his  opponent jose antonio cast the far-right  candidate was is still an admirer of  the dictatorship of agusto pinochet what  was made during the dictatorship so this  is indeed something very interesting  it's a polarizing election it was a  polarizing day as well something that  many call the most important  presidential election since the return  to democracy in 1990  from santiago chile dw correspondent  benjamin alvarez-gruber many thanks  well let's bring in latin america expert  gunther myhold here he works at the  german institute for international and  security affairs here in berlin good to  have you with us now gabrielle borich is  not only the youngest candidate who ever  got elected in chile he also got more  votes than any other presidential  candidate in the history of the country  how do you explain his success  he's a representative of a new  generation he has about half of the age  of the actual president sebastian pinera  and he is a representative  of the student and social movement of  the  2019  demonstrations on the streets  and at the same time he represents a new  generation of political actors because  all the old parties of the transition  period of the chilean democracy have  been losing place and he is now  on the front of a social movement  government which will generate new  topics new electoral  uh perspectives for the people  so a new generation of leadership but  the challenges that have been plaguing  chile are still very much the same what  are those challenges that the new  president will have to prioritize  he has been  given a clear program to the people he  wants a reform of the  development model and goodbye to  neoliberal model where chile has been  the forerunner in the years  of the past decades and he wants to  generate a new type of politics this  means that he has to  come to a model to include the citizens  on the street to generate new forums to  make policy and at the same time to have  the space with the constitutional  assembly to  write a new constitution  okay there are pressing uh social and  economic challenges in the country as  well he's a new genera he represents a  new generation you say well other  countries in latin america are also  struggling they've been looking for  changes too  oh they've been watching this election  how important is this election for other  countries in the region what what signal  does it give to  them at the first side it seems that the  progressive part of the political  systems in latin america will be  strengthened by this but we must be  clear that there is a heterogeneous  cooperation between these different  countries lula is the old generation  porridge is the new generation though i  think we will have a push to a left  oriented governments but at the same  time we have to be clear that they will  be interested in quite different uh  questions in the internal side and  porridge will have some sort of new  leadership that the old man can't  represent  gunter myholt from the german institute  for international security affairs thank  you very much  you're welcome
 

